{"question": "A 50-year-old woman with Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) presents to your clinic with complaints of left eye pain and reduced vision. She notes that she was in her usual state of health until 3 days prior to presentation when she noticed some mild tenderness around her left eye, unrelieved by any pain relievers. This morning when she woke up she was unable to see out of her left eye and made an appointment to come see you. Other than SLE, her medical history is unremarkable. She has had no additional symptoms other than pain and vision loss. Her vital signs are all within normal limits. On exam, she has no vision in her left eye, but 20/30 visual acuity in her right eye. When you shine a light in her left eye there is no response. What response would you expect when you shine a light in her right eye?", "options": {"A": "Bilateral mydriasis", "B": "Bilateral miosis", "C": "Miosis of the right eye only", "D": "Miosis of the left eye only", "E": "Mydriasis of the left eye"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Ophthalmology", "Rheumatology", "Neurology", "Internal Medicine", "Emergency Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Neuro-Ophthalmology", "Rheumatology"], "question_analyses": {"Ophthalmology": "In the scenario presented, the patient's symptoms and examination findings suggest a condition known as **retinal artery occlusion** (RAO) or **central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO)** specifically, given the sudden onset of painless vision loss in one eye. The absence of a pupillary light reflex (no response when a light is shone in the left eye) is a hallmark sign of CRAO, indicating that the retina is not receiving adequate blood flow and is therefore not functioning properly. When you shine a light in the right eye, you would expect a normal pupillary light reflex. This means that the pupil in the right eye should constrict when light is shone into it, and the left pupil should also constrict due to the consensual response (where 